  "result": "There is a particular kind of quiet that arrives just before rain. Birds lower their voices. Leaves turn their pale undersides toward the sky. Even the streets seem to wait.\n\nWe rarely notice how much of life is made of waiting: for news, for courage, for water to boil, for a wound to become a memory. We tend to treat these intervals as empty space, as though real life exists only in arrivals and decisions. But perhaps waiting is not the gap between events. Perhaps it is an event of its own\u2014a slow rearrangement of attention.\n\nA storm gathers by degrees. Pressure shifts. Clouds accumulate. Somewhere far away, the first drop falls.\n\nThen another.\n\nAnd suddenly the world is no longer waiting.",
